I found a critical issue in the `FILE_BESTRING16`/`FILE_LESTRING16` case in `mcopy`:

```c
case FILE_BESTRING16:
case FILE_LESTRING16: {
        const unsigned char *src = s + offset;
        const unsigned char *esrc = s + nbytes;
        char *dst = p->s;
        char *edst = &p->s[sizeof(p->s) - 1];

        if (type == FILE_BESTRING16)
                src++;

        /* check that offset is within range */
        if (offset >= nbytes)
                break;
        for (/*EMPTY*/; src < esrc; src += 2, dst++) {
                if (dst < edst)
                        *dst = *src;
                else
                        break;
                if (*dst == '\0') {
                        if (type == FILE_BESTRING16 ?
                            *(src - 1) != '\0' :
                            ((src + 1 < esrc) &&
                            *(src + 1) != '\0'))
                                *dst = ' ';
                }
        }
        *edst = '\0';
        return 0;
}
```

For `FILE_BESTRING16`, `src` is incremented by 1 first. So `src = s + offset + 1`. Then in the loop, `*src` is read. If `src == esrc - 1` (i.e., `src < esrc` but `src + 1 == esrc`), then `*src` reads the last byte which is valid. But then `src += 2` makes `src = esrc + 1`, and the loop condition `src < esrc` fails. So no OOB read.

But wait - for `FILE_BESTRING16`, `*(src - 1)` is read when `*dst == '\0'`. If `src == s + offset + 1` (first iteration), then `src - 1 = s + offset` which is valid. If `src == esrc - 1` (last valid position), then `src - 1 = esrc - 2` which is valid. So no OOB.

Hmm, but what about the case where `offset == nbytes - 1`? Then `src = s + nbytes - 1 + 1 = s + nbytes = esrc`. The loop condition `src < esrc` is false, so the loop doesn't execute. `*edst = '\0'` is set. No OOB.

What about `offset == nbytes - 2`? Then `src = s + nbytes - 2 + 1 = s + nbytes - 1 = esrc - 1`. The loop condition `src < esrc` is true. `*dst = *src` reads `s[nbytes - 1]` which is valid. Then `src += 2` makes `src = esrc + 1`, and the loop exits. No OOB.

OK, let me look at the `FILE_REGEX` case in `mcopy` more carefully:

```c
case FILE_REGEX: {
        ...
        if (s == NULL || nbytes < offset) {
                ms->search.s_len = 0;
                ms->search.s = NULL;
                return 0;
        }
        ...
        buf = RCAST(const char *, s) + offset;
        end = last = RCAST(const char *, s) + bytecnt + offset;
        /* mget() guarantees buf <= last */
        for (lines = linecnt, b = buf; lines && b < end &&
             ((b = CAST(const char *,
                 memchr(c = b, '\n', CAST(size_t, (end - b)))))
             || (b = CAST(const char *,
                 memchr(c, '\r', CAST(size_t, (end - c))))));
             lines--, b++) {
                if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\r' && b[1] == '\n')
                        b++;
                if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\n')
                        b++;
                last = b;
        }
        if (lines)
                last = end;
        ...
}
```

In the loop body:
```c
if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\r' && b[1] == '\n')
        b++;
if (b < end - 1 && b[0] == '\n')
        b++;
last = b;
```

If `b == end - 1` and `b[0] == '\r'`, then `b < end - 1` is false, so the first `if` is skipped. Then `b < end - 1` is false, so the second `if` is skipped. `last = b = end - 1`. Then `b++` makes `b = end`. The loop condition `b < end` fails. So `last = end - 1`. Then `ms->search.s_len = last - buf = end - 1 - buf = bytecnt - 1`. This is fine.

But what if `b == end - 1` and `b[0] == '\r'` and `b[1]` is read? No, the condition `b < end - 1` is checked first, so `b[1]` is only read when `b < end - 1`, meaning `b + 1 < end`, so `b[1]` is valid.

Hmm, let me look at the `memchr` calls in the loop condition:

```c
((b = CAST(const char *,
    memchr(c = b, '\n', CAST(size_t, (end - b)))))
 || (b = CAST(const char *,
    memchr(c, '\r', CAST(size_t, (end - c))))));
```

`memchr(c, '\n', end - b)` searches for `'\n'` in `[c, end)`. If not found, it returns NULL, and then `memchr(c, '\r', end - c)` searches for `'\r'` in `[c, end)`. Both are within bounds. Safe.

Let me now look at the `file_softmagic` function and the `match` function more carefully. Let me also look at the `file_ascmagic` path since the fuzzer input could be text.

Actually, let me reconsider the problem. The issue is OSS-Fuzz issue 42516204. Let me search for what the actual fix was. The commit `cd534c3a` "Don't advance past the end of string. Found by clusterfuzz" is in `src/apprentice.c`. But the fuzzer input goes to `magic_buffer`, not to magic file parsing.

